PHASE 13 — APPLICATION UX AND DESIGN REVIEW

Compare the existing experience with established rental-platform UX practices.

Do not copy another platform’s branding or proprietary design.

13.1 Visual hierarchy

Evaluate:

* Page header.
* Brand consistency.
* Progress indicator.
* Step titles.
* Form sections.
* Labels.
* Input sizing.
* Spacing.
* Typography.
* Buttons.
* Summary cards.
* Fee breakdown.
* Consent sections.
* Error messages.
* Confirmation state.
* Mobile viewport.
* Desktop layout.
* Accessibility.

The design should help applicants understand where they are, what information is required, what the fee will be, and what happens next.

13.2 Form usability

Evaluate:

* Whether the applicant knows how long the form will take.
* Whether the progress indicator is accurate.
* Whether required fields are clearly marked.
* Whether optional fields are distinguishable.
* Whether errors appear beside the relevant fields.
* Whether entered information is preserved after errors.
* Whether the form prevents accidental double submission.
* Whether the fee summary updates when applicant details change.
* Whether the user can review all information before submission.
* Whether the user can correct an error without starting over.
* Whether consent text is understandable.
* Whether the next step after submission is explicit.

Do not use disabled controls without explaining why they are disabled.

Do not rely only on color to communicate status.

Use accessible focus states, labels, keyboard navigation, and appropriate screen-reader announcements.

13.3 Mobile-first requirements

Test realistic mobile widths, especially small phone screens.

Investigate:

* Horizontal overflow.
* Clipped buttons.
* Small touch targets.
* Keyboard obstruction.
* Date-picker behavior.
* Dropdown usability.
* Long policy text.
* Fee-summary visibility.
* Progress-indicator wrapping.
* Co-applicant field expansion.
* Error-message placement.
* Language switching.
* Submission progress.

Keep the existing project’s mobile-first design rules.

13.4 Review and confirmation screen

Before submission, provide a clear summary of:

* Property.
* Primary applicant.
* Additional applicants and their roles.
* Important application details.
* Application fee per applicable adult.
* Total fee.
* Credit or waiver, if applicable.
* Required acknowledgements.
* What happens after submission.
* Whether payment is still outstanding.
* Whether the application has been submitted successfully.

Do not imply that an application has been approved merely because the form was submitted.

13.5 Confirmation state

After successful submission, explain:

* That the application was received.
* The application reference.
* The selected property.
* Whether a fee remains outstanding.
* Whether additional applicant steps remain.
* What happens next.
* How the applicant will be contacted.
* How to obtain help.

The confirmation page must reflect the actual backend response.

Do not display success if the backend has not confirmed persistence.

If the result is uncertain because of a network timeout, reconcile the submission before inviting the applicant to submit again.

