PHASE 14 — SECURITY, PRIVACY, AND SENSITIVE APPLICANT DATA

Rental applications can contain highly sensitive personal information.

Audit:

* Applicant data collection.
* Date of birth.
* Contact details.
* Income and employment information.
* Sensitive identifiers.
* Consent records.
* Local storage.
* Database access.
* Administrative permissions.
* Email content.
* Error reporting.
* Logs.
* Signing tokens.
* Tenant portal links.
* File attachments, if any.

14.1 Minimize exposure

Determine whether each sensitive field is necessary and whether it is exposed to the correct audience.

Do not unnecessarily include sensitive identifiers, full application details, or private documents in emails.

Do not log raw application payloads unless there is a justified, appropriately protected operational need.

Do not place privileged credentials in frontend code.

14.2 Authorization

Verify that:

* Applicants can access only their own application information.
* A co-applicant cannot access another household’s unrelated application.
* A signer can only sign the correct document.
* Administrative operations require appropriate authorization.
* Public property pages cannot expose applicant information.
* Client-controlled application IDs do not bypass access checks.
* Unauthenticated requests cannot retrieve private records.
* Sensitive data is not exposed through verbose error responses.

Do not treat an unguessable ID or token as a substitute for a complete authorization design.

14.3 Safe handling of stored data

Inspect:

* Row Level Security.
* Database grants.
* Edge Function authorization.
* Token generation and expiration.
* Local-storage behavior.
* Retention and deletion workflows.
* Backup implications.
* Error reporting.
* Email logs.
* Audit logs.

Do not delete or migrate production data as part of the audit.

Document data-retention questions requiring a business or legal decision.

